\title{LaTeX Math Style Guide}
\author{Ivan Jacob Agaloos Pesigan}
\date{}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

One equation should render the same everywhere it is pasted:
\LaTeX{} (pdf\LaTeX{}), the Rd PDF manual, pkgdown (KaTeX),
Quarto and R Markdown (MathJax), and Beamer.
Agents follow \texttt{.setup/ai/style/latex-math.md},
which follows this guide and is changed together with it.

\section{Form}

What a symbol means can change from paper to paper;
each project lists its symbols in \texttt{.setup/ai/project.md}.
Its form does not change, and follows the conventions of linear algebra,
statistics, and calculus.

\begin{itemize}
	\item Scalars and elements: plain italic (\(a_{12, i}\), \(\alpha\)).
	\item Vectors: bold lowercase (\(\boldsymbol{y}\), \(\boldsymbol{\eta}\)).
	\item Matrices: bold uppercase (\(\boldsymbol{B}\), \(\boldsymbol{\Phi}\)).
	\item Named exception: the Wiener process \(\boldsymbol{W}\) is a vector in bold uppercase,
	the usual form for a stochastic process.
\end{itemize}

\section{Bold}

\begin{itemize}
	\item \texttt{\textbackslash boldsymbol\{\}} for every vector and matrix, Latin and Greek.
	\item Do not write \texttt{\textbackslash mathbf} or \texttt{\textbackslash bm} in sources.
	MathJax has no \texttt{\textbackslash bm}, and \texttt{\textbackslash mathbf}
	prints upright on the web but italic in the PDF.
	The preamble keeps its remap to \texttt{\textbackslash bm} for better PDF glyphs,
	so this rule moves no glyph in a PDF.
	\item Scalars and elements stay plain italic (\(a_{12, i}\)).
\end{itemize}

\begin{Verbatim}
\boldsymbol{x}_{i, t}  \boldsymbol{A}  \boldsymbol{\Sigma}  a_{12, i}
\end{Verbatim}

\section{Symbols}

\begin{itemize}
	\item Transpose: \texttt{\^{}\{\textbackslash prime\}}
	(not \texttt{\^{}T}, \texttt{\^{}\{\textbackslash top\}}, or \texttt{'}).
	\item Operators: \texttt{\textbackslash operatorname\{\}}
	(\texttt{vec}, \texttt{vech}, \texttt{tr}, \texttt{diag}, \texttt{Cov}),
	never \texttt{\textbackslash text} or \texttt{\textbackslash mathrm}.
	\item Subscripts in braces, with a space after commas:
	\texttt{\textbackslash boldsymbol\{x\}\_\{i, t\}}.
	\item \texttt{\textbackslash left( \textbackslash right)} around groups of more than one symbol,
	and \texttt{\textbackslash !} before the \texttt{\textbackslash left(} of a function.
	\item \texttt{\textbackslash mathbb\{E\}} and \texttt{\textbackslash mathbb\{V\}}
	for expectation and variance.
	\item The differential is upright, as in ISO 80000-2, with a thin space before it:
	\texttt{\textbackslash , \textbackslash mathrm\{d\} t}, and
	\texttt{\textbackslash frac\{\textbackslash mathrm\{d\} y\}\{\textbackslash mathrm\{d\} t\}}
	for a derivative. This is the exception to the operator rule.
	The partial derivative stays \texttt{\textbackslash partial}.
\end{itemize}

\begin{Verbatim}
\mathcal{N} \! \left( \boldsymbol{0}, \boldsymbol{\Xi} \right)
\operatorname{vec} \! \left( \boldsymbol{A} \right)
\boldsymbol{A}^{\prime}
\end{Verbatim}

\section{Display Equations}

\begin{itemize}
	\item \texttt{equation} when the equation is referred to, \texttt{equation*} otherwise.
	\item A short equation stays on one line.
	\item A long one breaks before \texttt{=}, \texttt{+}, and \texttt{\textbackslash sim},
	and at commas between parts: one piece per line, indented with a tab.
\end{itemize}

\begin{Verbatim}
\begin{equation}
	\boldsymbol{\eta}_{i, t}
	=
	\boldsymbol{\alpha}
	+ \boldsymbol{B} \boldsymbol{\eta}_{i, t - 1}
	+ \boldsymbol{\zeta}_{i, t},
	\quad
	\boldsymbol{\zeta}_{i, t}
	\sim \mathcal{N} \! \left( \boldsymbol{0}, \boldsymbol{\Psi} \right)
	\label{eq:template-var}
\end{equation}
\end{Verbatim}

\section{Macros}

None.
Plain, portable commands only.
Do not use \texttt{\textbackslash newcommand}, \texttt{\textbackslash renewcommand},
or \texttt{\textbackslash DeclareMathOperator} outside \texttt{.setup/latex/preamble}.

\section{Portable Math}

Use only commands that work in all of the contexts above:
the Greek and Latin letters that \LaTeX{} defines,
\texttt{\textbackslash boldsymbol},
\texttt{\textbackslash mathcal}, \texttt{\textbackslash mathbb}, \texttt{\textbackslash mathrm\{d\}},
\texttt{\textbackslash operatorname},
\texttt{\textbackslash frac}, \texttt{\textbackslash sqrt}, \texttt{\textbackslash exp}, \texttt{\textbackslash log},
\texttt{\textbackslash sum}, \texttt{\textbackslash prod}, \texttt{\textbackslash int},
\texttt{\textbackslash sim}, \texttt{\textbackslash mid}, \texttt{\textbackslash ldots},
\texttt{\textbackslash left}, \texttt{\textbackslash right}, \texttt{\textbackslash quad},
\texttt{\textbackslash ,},
\texttt{\textbackslash hat}, \texttt{\textbackslash tilde}, \texttt{\textbackslash bar},
and the environments \texttt{pmatrix} and \texttt{aligned}.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\hline
Not this & This \\
\hline
\texttt{\textbackslash Beta}, \texttt{\textbackslash Alpha} (not \LaTeX{}) & \texttt{\textbackslash mathrm\{B\}}, \texttt{\textbackslash mathrm\{A\}} \\
\texttt{\textbackslash argmax}, \texttt{\textbackslash argmin} & \texttt{\textbackslash operatorname*\{arg\textbackslash ,max\}} \\
\texttt{\textbackslash bm}, \texttt{\textbackslash mathbf} & \texttt{\textbackslash boldsymbol} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

rProject's test page (\texttt{.setup/quarto/math/math.qmd}) renders these commands with MathJax,
and this guide renders them in the PDF,
so a change in a renderer shows up.

\section{Shared Notation}

This table holds the default symbols across projects.
A paper can give a symbol another meaning:
its \texttt{.setup/ai/project.md} (``Models and notation'')
lists its own symbols and any departures, in the same form.
Existing packages and papers keep their symbols until they are next reused.
Elements of a vector or matrix use the plain letter.
The subscripts \(i\) and \(t\) are the person and the time.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Symbol & Source & Meaning \\
\hline
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{Measurement}} \\
\texttt{\textbackslash boldsymbol\{y\}\_\{i, t\}} & \(\boldsymbol{y}_{i, t}\) & observed variables \\
\texttt{\textbackslash boldsymbol\{\textbackslash eta\}\_\{i, t\}} & \(\boldsymbol{\eta}_{i, t}\) & latent variables \\
\texttt{\textbackslash boldsymbol\{\textbackslash nu\}} & \(\boldsymbol{\nu}\) & measurement intercepts \\
\texttt{\textbackslash boldsymbol\{\textbackslash Lambda\}} & \(\boldsymbol{\Lambda}\) & factor loadings \\
\texttt{\textbackslash boldsymbol\{\textbackslash varepsilon\}\_\{i, t\}} & \(\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}_{i, t}\) & measurement errors \\
\texttt{\textbackslash boldsymbol\{\textbackslash Theta\}} & \(\boldsymbol{\Theta}\) & covariance of the measurement errors \\
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{Discrete time (VAR)}} \\
\texttt{\textbackslash boldsymbol\{\textbackslash alpha\}} & \(\boldsymbol{\alpha}\) & intercepts \\
\texttt{\textbackslash boldsymbol\{B\}} & \(\boldsymbol{B}\) & transition matrix \\
\texttt{\textbackslash boldsymbol\{\textbackslash zeta\}\_\{i, t\}} & \(\boldsymbol{\zeta}_{i, t}\) & process noise \\
\texttt{\textbackslash boldsymbol\{\textbackslash Psi\}} & \(\boldsymbol{\Psi}\) & covariance of the process noise \\
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{Continuous time}} \\
\texttt{\textbackslash boldsymbol\{\textbackslash iota\}} & \(\boldsymbol{\iota}\) & intercepts (linear SDE) \\
\texttt{\textbackslash boldsymbol\{\textbackslash mu\}} & \(\boldsymbol{\mu}\) & set point (Ornstein-Uhlenbeck) \\
\texttt{\textbackslash boldsymbol\{\textbackslash Phi\}} & \(\boldsymbol{\Phi}\) & drift matrix \\
\texttt{\textbackslash boldsymbol\{\textbackslash Sigma\}} & \(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}\) & diffusion covariance \\
\texttt{\textbackslash boldsymbol\{W\}\_\{i, t\}} & \(\boldsymbol{W}_{i, t}\) & Wiener process \\
\texttt{\textbackslash Delta t} & \(\Delta t\) & time interval \\
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{Continuous-time mediation}} \\
\texttt{\textbackslash exp \textbackslash ! \textbackslash left( \textbackslash Delta t \textbackslash boldsymbol\{\textbackslash Phi\} \textbackslash right)} & \(\exp \! \left( \Delta t \boldsymbol{\Phi} \right)\) & total effects \\
\texttt{\textbackslash boldsymbol\{D\}\_\{\textbackslash boldsymbol\{m\}\}} & \(\boldsymbol{D}_{\boldsymbol{m}}\) & removes the mediators, for the direct effects \\
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{Cross-sectional mediation}} \\
\texttt{X}, \texttt{M}, \texttt{Y} & \(X\), \(M\), \(Y\) & predictor, mediator, outcome \\
\texttt{\textbackslash alpha}, \texttt{\textbackslash beta} & \(\alpha\), \(\beta\) & paths \(X \rightarrow M\), \(M \rightarrow Y\) \\
\texttt{\textbackslash tau\^{}\{\textbackslash prime\}} & \(\tau^{\prime}\) & direct effect \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The linear stochastic differential equation and the Ornstein-Uhlenbeck model are

\begin{Verbatim}
\mathrm{d} \boldsymbol{\eta}_{i, t}
=
\left( \boldsymbol{\iota} + \boldsymbol{\Phi} \boldsymbol{\eta}_{i, t} \right) \, \mathrm{d} t
+ \boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{\frac{1}{2}} \, \mathrm{d} \boldsymbol{W}_{i, t}

\mathrm{d} \boldsymbol{\eta}_{i, t}
=
\boldsymbol{\Phi} \left( \boldsymbol{\eta}_{i, t} - \boldsymbol{\mu} \right) \, \mathrm{d} t
+ \boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{\frac{1}{2}} \, \mathrm{d} \boldsymbol{W}_{i, t}
\end{Verbatim}

\section{Tools}

\texttt{make lint} reports (never fails on) bold, portable math,
macros, and symbols, in \LaTeX{} sources, roxygen \texttt{\textbackslash eqn\{\}} and \texttt{\textbackslash deqn\{\}},
and Markdown math.

\nocite{RCoreTeam-2026}

